[A] <b>"Let him rouse himself like a lion," etc.</b> Later authorities write in the name of the Zohar: A person should not say, "I am lying on my bed and I will study"; rather he must stand and dress, because of "Prepare to meet your God, Israel," and if possible he should stand on his feet so that he does not fall into sleep—very good. They also wrote that one should connect morning and evening, day and night, in Torah or in prayer. Reshit Chokhmah and Shlah and other works of ethics elaborated greatly on the mitzvah of rising at midnight and the punishment of one who neglects it—see there. They also wrote: Whoever stands at midnight to study should engage in Oral Torah. And the time of midnight: Shaarei Tziyyon in the name of the Zohar—whether in summer when nights are short or in winter when nights are long—from twelve proportional hours; midnight is after six hours from the actual beginning of night. And so it is reasonable that night always begins for everyone specifically from the actual beginning of night, as it is written, "and there was evening and there was morning." And in Sefer Derekh Chokhmah p. 46 it appears that he understood that night begins in summer season some hours before night—and therefore he asked on him: how do we pray Minchah in summer within six hours before midnight of night, and also why melachah is not forbidden in summer on Erev Shabbat from six hours before night. He did not sharpen the difficulty. It is further explained in Zohar and Shaarei Tziyyon that in winter, after twelve hours from the beginning of night, even though the night still truly belongs to the day both for midnight and for watches. And what is difficult: if so, we would permit praying Shacharit before daylight, and in summer one would not pray Shacharit until after twelve hours from the start of night. It seems to me that the law of prayer is different, for prayer was established according to times; Shacharit is because we merit to see the day, as will be explained below; and therefore we require that we have "day." And perhaps there are further hidden reasons. In any case it seems to me to prove from the Talmud at the beginning of Berakhot that it argues with the Zohar and holds that we count night, whether summer or winter, by proportional hours, and midnight is truly half the night always, for it asks, "If the end of a watch is considered…"—end of the last watch as a sign—why do I need daytime?—until here. And if according to the Zohar there is no difficulty, for we need a sign for long nights. Also what does the Talmud ask—Scripture says, "At midnight I rise," and "my eyes preceded the watches"—even though one rises at midnight, that is, after six hours from the start of night, nevertheless there are two watches until daytime in long nights, and understand this. Lechem Chamudot, chapter "HaRo'eh," wrote: even though one must rise promptly without laziness, nevertheless he should pause a little and not stand up suddenly immediately after sleep, for the master said at the end of chapter Mefanin—five things are close to death: eat and stand, drink and stand, sleep and stand, bloodletting and stand, marital relations and stand—except wait a moment and then rise:
